<article class="env-lemma" id="02OU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02OU">Lemma <span data-tag="02OU">31.24.5</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes. In each of the following cases pullbacks of meromorphic functions are defined. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
every weakly associated point of $X$ maps to a generic point of an irreducible component of $Y$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$, $Y$ are integral and $f$ is dominant, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is integral and the generic point of $X$ maps to a generic point of an irreducible component of $Y$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is reduced and every generic point of every irreducible component of $X$ maps to the generic point of an irreducible component of $Y$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is locally Noetherian, and any associated point of $X$ maps to a generic point of an irreducible component of $Y$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is locally Noetherian, has no embedded points and any generic point of an irreducible component of $X$ maps to the generic point of an irreducible component of $Y$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f$ is flat. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The question is local on $X$ and $Y$. Hence we reduce to the case where $X = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$, $Y = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ and $f$ is given by a ring map $\varphi : R \to A$. By the characterization of regular sections of the structure sheaf in Lemma <a href="/tag/01WZ" data-tag="01WZ">31.15.7</a> we have to show that $R \to A$ maps nonzerodivisors to nonzerodivisors. Let $t \in R$ be a nonzerodivisor. 
    <p>
      If $R \to A$ is flat, then $t : R \to R$ being injective shows that $t : A \to A$ is injective. This proves (7). 
    <p>
      In the other cases we note that $t$ is not contained in any of the minimal primes of $R$ (because every element of a minimal prime in a ring is a zerodivisor). Hence in case (1) we see that $\varphi (t)$ is not contained in any weakly associated prime of $A$. Thus this case follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/05C3" data-tag="05C3">10.66.7</a>. Case (5) is a special case of (1) by Lemma <a href="/tag/05AR" data-tag="05AR">31.5.8</a>. Case (6) follows from (5) and the definitions. Case (4) is a special case of (1) by Lemma <a href="/tag/0EME" data-tag="0EME">31.5.12</a>. Cases (2) and (3) are special cases of (4). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>